succeeded, and every path has a live successor.\n\nAsserts 528a17f5 and ada211f6 are honest history of past acts and need nothing.\n\nTHE REAL WORK THAT REMAINS, if someone wants to own it, is a fresh request against current main, not a revival — reusing this promise and report chain would carry the world-stale artifact lineage into the result and make it unmergeable for exactly the reason b39aaf1a is. Scope: publish at docs/reference/performance.md a versioned two-envelope table naming PREVIEW and FIRST-PRODUCTION by sequence depth, actor count, dependency fan-out and checkpoint size, and a target table giving cold restart with a checkpoint, cold verify without one, warm-status latency, checkpoint bytes on disk and actor-count cost an explicit number per envelope with the reason for that number; leave the three axes main already carries unchanged and un-relitigated; measure the five new axes with the existing internal/perfscenario harness under performance/contract-v2.json, retaining raw samples under performance/retained/\u003crun-id\u003e/; publish the already-durable restart figures from e2b15773 and 7994ba10 rather than re-deriving them; label every row measured or modeled; file a bounded child request for each miss; correct performance.md:227-228; and publish an exact artifact at path docs resting only on live current-world anchors.\n\nI am not filing that as a request today. This log already carries more unclaimed requests than it closes, and a scope statement with no owner is worth more in an assert than in a row that reads as work someone promised to route.\n\nHousekeeping: branch request/projection-scale-stress and its prunable worktree can go once the rows are retired."}